# The corpus is the answer key, and the answer key is a byte-for-byte copy of an upstream git
# blob. `git config core.autocrlf` is `true` on the Windows machine this project is developed on,
# which on 2026-09-01 meant that eight of seventeen corpus-2 cases were checked out with CRLF line
# endings and no longer matched the blob they are supposed to be. Nothing could catch it until
# corpus2/manifest.json started carrying content hashes; then it was obvious within minutes.
# That is error 34.
#
# `-text` switches newline conversion off entirely for these paths, on every platform, so what a
# scanner reads is what the upstream repository holds and the recorded sha256 and git blob id are
# reproducible on Windows, Linux and macOS alike.
corpus2/** -text

# Findings files, run logs and raw scanner output are records of what a tool actually emitted.
# Rewriting their line endings on checkout would change their hashes for no reason.
raw/** -text
